WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.980
A movie that we watch in the TV shows us from 25 to about 60 images per second.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.580
In computer graphics these images are
referred to as frames.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.860
A slow-motion camera can capture up to the order of thousands of frames per second

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:20.390
providing breathtaking footage like
this. One can quickly discover the beauty

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.840
of even the most ordinary, mundane
moments of nature.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.620
But, if you think this is slow motion
then take a look at this.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.660
Computer graphics researchers have been working
on a system that is able to capture one

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.980
trillion frames per second.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:43.800
How much is that exactly? Well, it means
that if every single person who lives on

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.239
Earth would be able to help us, then
every single one of us would have to

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:54.660
take about 140 photographs in one second. And we would then need to add all of

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.600
these photographs up to obtain only one
second of footage.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:05.300
What is all this good for? Well, for example, capturing light as an electromagnetic

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:11.760
wave as it hits and travels along
objects in space like the wall that you see here.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.820
Physicists use to say that there is a
really really short instance of time

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:22.260
when you stand in front of the mirror.
You look at it and there is no mirror

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.700
image in. It it is completely black.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.710
What is this wizardry and how is this
possible? Since Einstein, we know that

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.450
the speed of light is finite.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:38.820
It is not instantaneous. It takes time to
travel from the light source, hit the

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:43.440
mirror, and end up hitting your eye for
you to see your mirror reflection.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:49.220
Researchers at MIT and the 
University of Zaragoza have captured this very moment.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.360
Take a look. It is an enlightening
experience.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.220
The paper is available in the
description box and it's a really

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:03.119
enjoyable read. A sizable portion of it
is understandable for everyone,

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.420
even without mathematical knowledge. All you need is just a little imagination

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.520
Thanks for watching and I'll see you
next week!

